<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/dna-heredity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    DNA is a two-stranded ladder twisted into a helix, and its elegance is its chemistry:
    <strong>A pairs only with T, G only with C</strong>. That one rule means each strand is a
    template for the other — the basis of copying, repairing and reading the molecule. Your
    genome is ~3 billion base pairs across 23 chromosome pairs, containing ~20,000
    protein-coding genes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from monk's garden to programmable editor — 150 years in ten steps" widget="timeline" config={dnaTimeline} />

  <h2>Mendel's ratios, explained</h2>
  <p>
    Crossing pure tall peas with pure short ones gave all tall offspring; self-crossing those
    gave a <strong>3:1</strong> ratio. The explanation: each plant carries two copies of every
    gene, one visible "dominant" and one hidden "recessive" — exactly what meiosis and DNA
    replication make physical. Traits like your blood group and eye colour run on the same
    arithmetic, though most human traits involve thousands of genes acting together.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the structure mattered</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Antiparallel strands + base pairing</strong> = a copying mechanism, which Watson and Crick announced in one of science's great understatements: "It has not escaped our notice…"</li>
    <li><strong>The sequence is information.</strong> Three DNA letters (a codon) specify one amino acid; chains of amino acids fold into proteins that do the cell's work.</li>
    <li><strong>Franklin's data was essential</strong> — her diffraction images measured the helix's diameter and spacing. History has been correcting her credit ever since.</li>
    <li><strong>CRISPR closes the loop:</strong> once you can read the code (1953–2003) and write it (2012–), heredity becomes engineering — with all the ethics that implies.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Watson & Crick, Nature (1953); Human Genome Project (Nature, 2003); Jinek et al., Science (2012).</p>
</TopicPage>
